@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "DISTINCT";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DISTINCT
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            The DISTINCT keyword is used to return only unique
            values from a column or combination of columns.
        </p>

        <p>
            When a table contains duplicate values and we want to
            display each value only once, we can use the
            <strong>DISTINCT</strong> keyword.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS DISTINCT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is DISTINCT?
        </h2>

        <p>
            DISTINCT removes duplicate values from the result
            returned by a SELECT query.
        </p>

        <p>
            It does not delete duplicate records from the actual
            table. It only removes duplicate values from the query
            result.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- BASIC SYNTAX -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Syntax
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT DISTINCT column_name
FROM table_name;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SAMPLE TABLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Table
        </h2>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                        <th>Course</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                        <td>.NET</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                        <td>Java</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                        <td>Python</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                        <td>.NET</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>105</td>
                        <td>Rohan</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                        <td>Java</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- WITHOUT DISTINCT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Without DISTINCT
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we want to display all cities from the Students
            table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT City
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The result may contain duplicate city names because
            multiple students can belong to the same city.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- WITH DISTINCT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Using DISTINCT
        </h2>

        <p>
            To display each city only once, use DISTINCT.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT DISTINCT City
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The result will contain only unique city names.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DISTINCT COURSE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DISTINCT with Course
        </h2>

        <p>
            DISTINCT can be applied to any column where we want
            unique values.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT DISTINCT Course
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns each course only once.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DISTINCT MULTIPLE COLUMNS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DISTINCT with Multiple Columns
        </h2>

        <p>
            DISTINCT can also be used with multiple columns.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT DISTINCT City, Course
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            In this case, SQL Server returns unique combinations of
            City and Course.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DISTINCT WITH WHERE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DISTINCT with WHERE
        </h2>

        <p>
            DISTINCT can be combined with the WHERE clause to filter
            records and then return unique values.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT DISTINCT City
FROM Students
WHERE Course = '.NET';</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns the unique cities of students enrolled in
            the .NET course.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DISTINCT WITH ORDER BY -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DISTINCT with ORDER BY
        </h2>

        <p>
            DISTINCT can also be used with ORDER BY to return unique
            values in a specific order.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT DISTINCT City
FROM Students
ORDER BY City ASC;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This returns unique city names in alphabetical order.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- DISTINCT VS GROUP BY -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            DISTINCT vs GROUP BY
        </h2>

        <p>
            DISTINCT is mainly used to remove duplicate values from
            a result set.
        </p>

        <p>
            GROUP BY is generally used when we want to group records
            and perform aggregate operations such as COUNT, SUM,
            AVG, MIN and MAX.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>SELECT DISTINCT City
FROM Students;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The above query simply returns unique cities.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DISTINCT returns unique values.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DISTINCT does not delete duplicate records from
                    the table.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DISTINCT can be used with one or multiple columns.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    DISTINCT can be combined with WHERE.
                </li>

                <li>
                    DISTINCT can be combined with ORDER BY.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned how the DISTINCT keyword
                is used to return unique values from SQL Server query
                results. We covered DISTINCT with single columns,
                multiple columns, WHERE and ORDER BY.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


